Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:  

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be giants or dwarfs depending on distance; Vmag alone is not strongly indicative because it depends on distance.  
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; giants tend to be luminous and often further away with smaller parallax, but this is not definitive alone.  
- e_Plx (parallax error): High error reduces reliability of distance and absolute magnitude; not directly indicative of giant status.  
- B-V (color index): Giants often have redder colors (higher B-V), but depends on spectral type.  
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in SpType (e.g., III, II) directly indicates giant or bright giant stars, while V indicates main sequence (dwarf) stars.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower absolute magnitude values (brighter intrinsically) compared to dwarfs, but this depends on calibration and distance accuracy.  

Step 2. 10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):  
- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, F6V, F8V, B8V, B9V, A0V, B9III/IV (if not clearly giant), etc. excluding III, II]  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) >= 18  
- B-V <= 0.6  
- Plx >= 4.0  
- Vmag <= 8.5 and Plx >= 4.0  
- SpType ends with "V" (main sequence)  
- Amag is within range of [18, 21]  
- B-V < 0.9 and SpType does not contain "III" or "II"  
- Plx is within range of [4, 35] and Amag >= 18  
- Vmag < 9 and SpType does not contain "III" or "II"  

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant star):  
- SpType contains "III" (e.g., K2III, G8III, K5III)  
- SpType contains "II" (e.g., K3II/III)  
- Amag < 17  
- B-V >= 0.9  
- Plx < 4.5  
- Vmag > 7.4 and SpType contains "III" or "II"  
- Amag is within range of [10, 17]  
- B-V is within range of [0.9, 1.6]  
- Plx is within range of [0.1, 4.5] and B-V > 0.9  
- SpType is in [A9III, K3II/III, G8III, K5III, K1III, G6/G8III]  

These conditions align with the examples provided and astrophysical knowledge that giants have spectral types with luminosity class III or II, redder colors, lower absolute magnitudes (brighter intrinsically), and typically smaller parallaxes due to being farther away.

