Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) may be giants because giants are intrinsically luminous, but apparent magnitude also depends on distance, so alone it is not decisive.
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means larger distance. Giants tend to be luminous and can be seen at larger distances with moderate brightness, so very small Plx with moderate Vmag may indicate a giant.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty, less relevant for classification but large errors reduce confidence.
- B-V (color index): Giants tend to have higher B-V (redder) as many giants are cooler stars, but some giants can be hotter. Color helps distinguish spectral types and luminosity classes.
- SpType (spectral type): This is the most direct indicator. Luminosity classes III, II, or III/IV indicate giant or subgiant stars; V indicates main sequence (dwarfs).
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitude values compared to dwarfs of the same spectral type.

